(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A lesion is oozing, vesicular and crusting. Which vehicle suits it?","choices":["The least drying end of the range","The middle of the range, regardless of the lesion","Vehicle choice is not linked to lesion morphology","The drying end of the range"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oozing, vesiculation and crusting are matched to drying vehicles; scaling, lichenification and xerosis to the least drying. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are the polymyxin black box warnings?","choices":["Ototoxicity, red man syndrome, and phlebitis","Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity, and neuromuscular blockade","Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and central nervous system effects","Thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome, and hypertensive crisis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the only warnings labels explicitly as black box, which is why they are worth holding separately from ordinary adverse effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A patient with asthma is started on propranolol and becomes wheezy. Why?","choices":["Muscarinic blockade","Beta-1 blockade","Beta-2 blockade","Alpha-1 blockade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Contraindicated in asthma and COPD. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 115"},{"q":"What is itraconazole used for?","choices":["Zygomycete infection including Mucor and Rhizopus","Systemic aspergillosis as first-line therapy","Blastomycosis, histoplasmosis and onychomycosis","Cryptococcal meningitis and relapse prophylaxis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It has an active metabolite and a wider spectrum than the ketoconazole it replaced. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Under drug factors, which organ-system toxicities are considered?","choices":["Gastrointestinal, renal, hepatic, ocular, reproductive, immunologic","Hematologic, renal, hepatic, endocrine, vascular, lymphatic","Gastrointestinal, integument, hematologic, central nervous system, hepatic, renal","Cardiac, pulmonary, endocrine, musculoskeletal, ocular, and dermatologic systems"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Six systems, and they map onto the monitoring parameters repeated for individual classes later. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Occlusion increases efficacy. What is flagged alongside it?","choices":["It raises the question of increased toxicity as well","It reliably shortens the required duration of therapy","It prevents the vehicle from exerting any therapeutic effect of its own","It makes regional variation in penetration irrelevant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The efficacy gain comes paired with a question about toxicity, not as a free benefit. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which mycoses are subcutaneous?","choices":["Aspergillosis, cryptococcosis and histoplasmosis","Oral and vaginal candidiasis","Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis","Tinea pedis, tinea cruris and tinea corporis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the middle tier, affecting skin layers rather than the surface or the internal organs. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Why does an oral corticosteroid course for atopic dermatitis need a taper?","choices":["To avoid precipitating drug-induced acne","To allow the topical steroid to be started safely","To reduce the risk of secondary infection","To prevent a flare-up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The taper protects against rebound rather than against withdrawal symptoms alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which terms mean the same as cholinergic antagonists?","choices":["Anticholinesterases and indirect-acting agents","Anticholinergics, parasympatholytics or antimuscarinics","Cholinomimetics and parasympathomimetics","Sympatholytics and adrenergic blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while the agonists are cholinomimetics or parasympathomimetics. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"When is acne treated topically rather than systemically?","choices":["Topical for adults, systemic for adolescents","Topical for the face, systemic for the trunk","Topical for mild to moderate disease, systemic for moderate to severe","Topical for inflammatory disease, systemic for comedonal disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severity is what moves a patient onto systemic therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which set of antivirals are prodrugs?","choices":["Valacyclovir, famciclovir, valganciclovir and oseltamivir","Acyclovir, penciclovir, ganciclovir and zanamivir","Amphotericin B, caspofungin and griseofulvin","Metronidazole, linezolid and daptomycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Improving oral absorption is a recurring design theme across the whole antiviral section. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"What happens when epinephrine is given to a patient on a beta blocker?","choices":["There is no clinically important interaction","Alpha effects are left unopposed","Both alpha and beta effects are abolished","The blood pressure falls sharply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the vasodilating beta side leaves only vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which tinea infection is commonly called ringworm?","choices":["Tinea pedis","Tinea cruris","Tinea unguium","Tinea corporis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Despite the name there is no worm involved; it describes the annular appearance on the body surface. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Norepinephrine extravasates and the skin blanches. What is the treatment?","choices":["Dantrolene","Pralidoxime","Phentolamine","Atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An alpha blocker reverses the intense local vasoconstriction before the skin sloughs. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which vehicle is the most drying, and which the least?","choices":["Creams are the most drying and gels the least","Powders are the most drying and lotions the least","Tinctures are the most drying and ointments the least","Ointments are the most drying and tinctures the least"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The list runs tinctures, wet dressings, lotions, gels, aerosols, powders, pastes, creams, foams, ointments. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which enzyme does acyclovir need, and which does oseltamivir target?","choices":["Acyclovir needs thymidine kinase; oseltamivir hits neuraminidase","Acyclovir hits neuraminidase; oseltamivir needs thymidine kinase","Both depend on viral thymidine kinase for activation","Both target viral neuraminidase at different sites"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one uses a viral enzyme to switch itself on, the other shuts a viral enzyme off. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Why is a selective beta-two agonist preferred over epinephrine in chronic asthma?","choices":["Greater potency at beta-two receptors","Oral availability","Absence of any tremor","Longer duration of action"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine reverses acute bronchospasm; albuterol covers chronic treatment. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which manifestations does cytomegalovirus cause?","choices":["Retinitis, oesophagitis and colitis","Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores and genital ulceration","Influenza-like illness with fever and myalgia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they occur chiefly in transplant recipients and other immunocompromised patients. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is carbachol used for, and what receptors does it hit?","choices":["Lowering intraocular pressure in glaucoma","Stimulating an atonic bladder, with muscarinic activity only","Treating xerostomia, with muscarinic activity only","Diagnosing myasthenia gravis, by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it causes miosis and spasm of accommodation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"An immunocompromised patient needs prophylaxis against Pneumocystis pneumonia. Which drug?","choices":["Doxycycline","Metronidazole","Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole","Azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Treatment and prophylaxis for Pneumocystis jirovecii. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 84"},{"q":"Why does cocaine exaggerate epinephrine's cardiovascular actions?","choices":["It increases receptor synthesis","It inhibits monoamine oxidase","It converts epinephrine to norepinephrine","It prevents reuptake"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so the transmitter persists at the receptor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What must a patient on metronidazole avoid?","choices":["Dairy products, because of chelation reducing absorption","Tyramine-containing foods, because of hypertensive crisis","Sun exposure, because of photosensitivity","Ethanol, because of a disulfiram-like reaction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the class's signature counselling point, alongside headache, nausea and vomiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which antiandrogen is used for acne?","choices":["Cyproterone","Spironolactone","Finasteride","Ketoconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a gel formulation is available alongside oral use. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which tetracycline precautions relate to children and pregnancy?","choices":["Grey baby syndrome — avoid in all trimesters","Teeth discoloration and skeletal effects; avoid under 8","Cartilage damage — avoid under 18 years old","Kernicterus; avoid in the first 30 days of life"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both follow from the drug binding calcium in developing bone and teeth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which tinea infection is commonly called jock itch?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Tinea pedis","Tinea unguium","Tinea cruris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, affecting the groin. The azoles are indicated across this whole tinea group. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Systemic aspergillosis unresponsive to itraconazole and amphotericin B. Which class?","choices":["Echinocandins","Polyenes","Allylamines","Mitotic inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — their listed role is largely salvage, in refractory aspergillosis, oesophageal candidiasis, and febrile neutropenia not responding to antibiotics. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which polyene treats oral candidiasis?","choices":["Amphotericin B deoxycholate","Natamycin","Caspofungin","Nystatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, available as oral preparations and a topical powder, with systemic effects limited by poor absorption. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects does acyclovir cause?","choices":["Seizures, delirium and tremor","Confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma","Peripheral neuropathy and tendon rupture","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside nausea, vomiting, rash and bone marrow suppression. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which viruses does oseltamivir target?","choices":["Influenza A only, year-round","Herpes simplex and varicella-zoster, circulating year-round","Cytomegalovirus in transplant recipients","Influenza A and B, in a season running roughly October to March"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and resistance has been becoming a problem over time. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"An aminopenicillin is added to warfarin. What is the interaction?","choices":["Abnormal prolongation of the prothrombin time","Reduced anticoagulant effect requiring an increased warfarin dose","No clinically meaningful interaction, since penicillins are renally cleared","Increased risk of tendon rupture when the two are combined"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulants head the list of aminopenicillin interactions. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does succinylcholine differ mechanistically from the nondepolarizing blockers?","choices":["It competitively prevents acetylcholine from binding without activating the receptor","It inhibits acetylcholinesterase in the junction","It blocks calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","It depolarizes the junction like acetylcholine but is not rapidly destroyed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Continued binding leaves the receptor unable to transmit further impulses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A patient on a monoamine oxidase inhibitor eats aged cheese and the pressure spikes. Why?","choices":["Beta-2 stimulation","Tyramine displaces norepinephrine","Direct alpha agonism","Cheese contains adrenaline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normally destroyed by MAO in the gut. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 101"},{"q":"What monitoring do the penicillinase-resistant penicillins need?","choices":["Renal function alone, since the class is cleared entirely by the kidney","Serum drug troughs drawn before the fourth dose","Creatine phosphokinase and muscle pain","Hepatic function, complete blood count, and signs or symptoms of anaphylaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note the dosing consideration for this class is hepatic rather than renal function, unlike most other penicillins. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Herpes simplex meningitis. Which type affects the meninges?","choices":["Type 2","Type 1","Neither type; meningitis is a cytomegalovirus manifestation","Neither type; meningitis is caused by varicella-zoster only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Type 2 covers genitals, rectum, hands or meninges; type 1 covers mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"In which patients is propranolol contraindicated?","choices":["Those recovering from myocardial infarction","Those with asthma or chronic lung disease","Those with hypertension","Those with migraine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because beta-two blockade causes bronchoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"What is flucytosine used against?","choices":["Helminths including roundworm and whipworm","Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida","Dermatophytes of the nails and scalp","Aspergillus and the Zygomycetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is given in combination rather than alone because resistance develops readily. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which two properties distinguish adapalene from tretinoin?","choices":["Adapalene is stable in sunlight and stable with benzoyl peroxide","Adapalene is a systemic agent and requires pregnancy testing","Adapalene is used only for psoriasis rather than acne","Adapalene must be applied only at night because it is photolabile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it also tends to be less irritating. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A poorly controlled diabetic develops a fungal infection. Which predisposing category?","choices":["Suppression of competing organisms","Metabolic abnormality","Immunodeficiency","Loss of barriers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — diabetes is the example for that category. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A patient on levofloxacin develops sudden posterior ankle pain. What is the concern?","choices":["Septic arthritis","Gout","Compartment syndrome","Achilles tendon rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Tendonitis and tendon rupture are the class risk. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 2-week-old has suspected sepsis. Which cephalosporin is preferred?","choices":["Cefazolin","Cefepime","Cefotaxime","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ceftriaxone cannot be used in the first 30 days of life. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the polymyxin mechanism of action?","choices":["A detergent action on the Gram-negative outer membrane","Inhibition of 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall","Binds 30S and blocks transfer RNA at the A site","Covalent inactivation of penicillin-binding proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why the spectrum is broad Gram-negative coverage — the target only exists there. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What must a patient do after handling a scopolamine patch?","choices":["Wash their hands thoroughly afterwards","Avoid showering for twenty-four hours so the patch adheres","Apply the patch to the same site each time to maintain levels","Take an antihistamine alongside it to prevent sedation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a small instruction that prevents an alarming symptom. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Where are alpha-one receptors found, against alpha-two?","choices":["Both exclusively on the postsynaptic membrane","Both exclusively within the central nervous system","Alpha-one postsynaptically, alpha-two presynaptically","Alpha-one presynaptically and alpha-two postsynaptically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and that presynaptic location is what makes an alpha-two agonist reduce sympathetic outflow. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A patient on gentamicin reports new ringing in the ears. Which toxicity is this?","choices":["Retinal toxicity","Ototoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Listed with nephrotoxicity for the class. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 68"},{"q":"Into which two groups do the direct-acting cholinergic agonists divide?","choices":["Catecholamines and noncatecholamines","Choline esters and naturally occurring alkaloids","Reversible and irreversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitors","Antimuscarinics and ganglionic blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The esters are synthetic apart from acetylcholine itself. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which effect do the alpha-adrenergic blocking agents share?","choices":["Reduced aqueous humour production","Reflex tachycardia","Reflex bradycardia","Bronchoconstriction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and their effects on blood pressure are more profound when the patient stands. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What are carbapenems drug of choice for?","choices":["Resistant Gram-negatives, ESBL producers, meningitis","MRSA bacteraemia, endocarditis and osteomyelitis","Uncomplicated urinary tract infection and outpatient sinusitis","Atypical pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma or Legionella"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Their resistance to hydrolysis by beta-lactamases is what makes them the answer to extended-spectrum producers. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A young woman has septic shock after a retained tampon. Which antibiotic binds the toxin?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Gentamicin","Clindamycin","Vancomycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Used for toxin-mediated disease because it binds the toxin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 80"},{"q":"At what dose does dopamine begin to cause vasoconstriction, and through which receptor?","choices":["At low doses, through alpha-one","At low doses, through beta-two","At any dose, through dopaminergic receptors","At very high doses, through alpha-one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, whereas its cardiac effect is beta-one and its renal effect dopaminergic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is amphotericin B's mechanism of action?","choices":["Forms pores in ergosterol membranes, leaking ions","It inhibits lanosterol demethylase, reducing ergosterol synthesis","It inhibits 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase, weakening the cell wall","A fungal enzyme converts it, then it blocks thymidylate synthase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The drug is derived from a soil Streptomycetaceae, and the leak is why electrolyte disturbance is such a prominent toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which set matches atropine's adverse effects?","choices":["Sedation, constipation and rebound hypertension","Dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention and constipation","Salivation, lacrimation, diarrhoea and bronchospasm","Fasciculations, weakness and hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with bradycardia at lower doses and tachycardia at higher ones. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do ganglionic blockers do, and why are they rarely used?","choices":["They act at the neuromuscular junction to relax skeletal muscle","They block alpha and beta receptors simultaneously","They block nicotinic receptors at sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia","They block muscarinic receptors only, sparing the sympathetic system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and blocking everything at once is precisely why they are rarely used clinically. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which electrolyte pattern accompanies amphotericin B?","choices":["Low sodium alone","High calcium","Low potassium and low magnesium","High potassium and high magnesium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which metabolic effect do the alpha-one blockers have?","choices":["Raised low-density lipoprotein and triglycerides","Fasting hypoglycaemia","No metabolic effect at all","Improved lipid profiles and glucose metabolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which contrasts with propranolol's worsening of both. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A patient on imipenem with renal impairment has a seizure. Is this expected?","choices":["Only with intrathecal use","Only in children","Yes, seizures are a known imipenem effect","No, carbapenems do not cause seizures"],"answer":2,"exp":"The unique side effect flagged for this agent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 44"},{"q":"Why is a sedating antihistamine advised for a child with atopic dermatitis?","choices":["To treat the secondary skin infections","To lower serum immunoglobulin E","To reduce night-time itching","To reverse the skin barrier dysfunction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sedating property is being used for the itch that disrupts sleep. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How is the nicotinic receptor structured?","choices":["An intracellular receptor that binds acetylcholine after it crosses the membrane","Five subunits forming a ligand-gated ion channel opened by two acetylcholine molecules","A seven-transmembrane receptor coupled to a G protein and a second messenger","An enzyme-linked receptor that phosphorylates intracellular targets on binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is found in the central nervous system, adrenal medulla, autonomic ganglia and neuromuscular junction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the interaction between benzoyl peroxide and tretinoin?","choices":["The two together cause systemic hypervitaminosis A","Benzoyl peroxide inactivates tretinoin","Benzoyl peroxide increases tretinoin absorption to toxic levels","Tretinoin prevents benzoyl peroxide from converting to benzoic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is why adapalene, which is stable with benzoyl peroxide, is highlighted separately. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which classes bind 50S, and which bind 30S?","choices":["50S: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines. 30S: macrolides, clindamycin","50S — fluoroquinolones, metronidazole; 30S — penicillins, cephalosporins","50S — vancomycin, daptomycin; 30S — linezolid, tigecycline","50S: macrolides, clindamycin. 30S: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The bacterial ribosome is 70S against the mammalian 80S, which is the basis for selectivity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What are albuterol's adverse effects?","choices":["Tremor, restlessness and anxiety","Sedation, dry mouth and constipation","Bradycardia and bronchoconstriction","Miosis, salivation and diarrhoea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, the familiar beta-two agonist profile. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"}]};
